body:
Also note with including windows, I think I mentioned a while back:

"always wrap expensive external headers"

A lot of projects wrap the specific windows functions / forward declarations they are using and put the actual `#include <windows.h>` in one specific cpp file, that way the huge compilation cost of `windows.h` is limited to a single cpp, and the whole build can be sped up significantly.

If you google for "windows.h wrappers c++" you might find some pointers with pre-made versions (but it's easy to make your own).

Grok gives this small example (not exactly what I'd use for the funcs, but illustrative):
<details>

```
#pragma once

// Forward declarations or minimal types only (no windows.h!)
using HWND     = void*;
using HINSTANCE = void*;
using DWORD    = unsigned long;
using BOOL     = int;

// No macros, no Win32 types in global namespace, no pollution

namespace my::win32
{
    HWND    CreateMyWindow(const char* title, int w, int h);
    void    MessageLoop();
    void    ShowErrorMessage(const char* msg);
    // ... only functions you actually call
}
```

```
#define WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN
#define NOMINMAX
#include <windows.h>

#include "MyWin32.h"

namespace my::win32
{
    HWND CreateMyWindow(const char* title, int w, int h)
    {
        // real Win32 code here
        WNDCLASS wc{};
        // ...
        return CreateWindow(...);
    }

    void MessageLoop()
    {
        MSG msg;
        while (GetMessage(&msg, nullptr, 0, 0))
        {
            TranslateMessage(&msg);
            DispatchMessage(&msg);
        }
    }

    // etc.
}
```
</details>


This is true also for any other large external headers (some do this for e.g. DirectX). You can even rig it so that it includes via cpp in `DEV` builds, and includes directly (incurring compilation cost) in release builds, if desired, but usually windows funcs are not bottlenecks.

(You probably can't do this for third party, but it's still usually a massive win.)

You can also link more modular parts of windows (but usually just one wrapper as above does the job):
https://aras-p.info/blog/2018/01/12/Minimizing-windows.h/

You can also just use precompiled headers, but the namespace pollution is high.
